# Four author identities appear in this history, and all four are the same person. A published history
# should not show them as four contributors, and the forge behind it would otherwise link four
# accounts on GitHub and Forgejo rather than one.
#
#   89  Piotr Adamiak <apjapj@gmail.com>
#   10  apjapj        <apjapj@forgejo>
#    2  Piotr         <apjapj@gmail.com>
#    1  apjapj        <apjapj@gmail.com>
#
# Two mistakes here are worth recording, because the first one was mine.
#
# A .mailmap line is  Proper Name <proper@email>  Commit Name <commit@email>, and git matches on BOTH
# the name and the email. An entry naming only the email does not work: `apjapj@forgejo
# apjapj@gmail.com` was written first, produced no change at all, and said so by git shortlog still
# showing two lines. An email-only mapping looks like it should work and silently does not.
#
# The second is that the *email* was never the problem worth solving. Three of the four identities share
# apjapj@gmail.com and differ only in the display name, so a reader would have seen "Piotr Adamiak",
# "Piotr" and "apjapj" as three contributors on the same address. That is the more visible half of the
# inconsistency and it is fixed below by the same file.
#
# Nothing here rewrites history. .mailmap is applied at display time by git log, git shortlog and the
# forge, so a fresh clone shows one contributor for 102 commits and no commit object is touched. Which
# display name and address to publish is the owner's decision; this file only makes the history say one
# thing instead of four.
Piotr Adamiak <apjapj@gmail.com> apjapj <apjapj@forgejo>
Piotr Adamiak <apjapj@gmail.com> Piotr <apjapj@gmail.com>
Piotr Adamiak <apjapj@gmail.com> apjapj <apjapj@gmail.com>